    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Jericho grew from a Natufian camping ground beside a spring into a settlement with a stone wall and tower, and later into a fortified Canaanite city that reached its greatest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> extent between 1700 and 1550 BCE.</li><li>The ancient city was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> or soon after and was occupied again from the 11th century BCE, and in the Hasmonean and Herodian periods a new Jericho of palaces and gardens was built on both banks of Wadi Qelt.</li><li>In the Byzantine period the town moved about 1,600 metres east to the site on which the modern town is centred, and it passed in turn to Muslim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a>, Ayyubid, Ottoman, British, Jordanian and Israeli rule.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A hundred men would have needed more than a hundred days to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> wall and tower of Jericho, which suggests some kind of social organisation; the tower stood over 8.5 metres high and held an internal staircase of 22 stone steps.</p></aside>
    </section>

